# crates/oc-ai/prompts/heading_roles/v1/grammar.gbnf
# Output: {"m":[{"c":<cluster>,"r":<role>},...],"h":[{"i":<probe>,"r":<role>},...]}
#
# "m" gives every style cluster of the payload one role. "h" labels every held-out line: the
# 8-10 runs sampled from those clusters and not shown as exemplars, which ride along in the
# same call (ARCHITECTURE 9.6, PIPELINE 8) and are the free oracle for "the clustering was
# wrong". No optional whitespace is admitted anywhere: the output is one canonical byte
# sequence per answer, which makes cassette diffs meaningful and saves output tokens.
#
# Arity. "m" holds at most 24 entries, which is inventory.max_clusters: the pre-gate refuses
# to call above that, so the grammar and the pre-gate agree by construction. "h" holds at
# most 10. The grammar cannot say "exactly n entries, each id once" for a runtime n; gate S
# checks that after parsing. `index` admits 0-99 because a two-range class is simpler to
# keep correct than a hand-rolled 0-23; the range check belongs to gate S too.
#
# A rule ends at the end of its line unless it is inside parentheses, which is why the role
# list is parenthesised. As bare continuation lines beginning with `|` - the form
# IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN Appendix A.2 prints - llama.cpp rejects the file.

root    ::= "{\"m\":" mapping ",\"h\":" holdout "}"
mapping ::= "[" entry ( "," entry ){0,23} "]"
entry   ::= "{\"c\":" index ",\"r\":" role "}"
holdout ::= "[" ( probe ( "," probe ){0,9} )? "]"
probe   ::= "{\"i\":" index ",\"r\":" role "}"
index   ::= [0-9] | [1-9] [0-9]
role    ::= (
      "\"chapter_heading\""
    | "\"part_heading\""
    | "\"section_heading\""
    | "\"subsection_heading\""
    | "\"running_head\""
    | "\"epigraph\""
    | "\"body\""
    | "\"caption\""
    | "\"other\""
  )
